   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Perceived stress (PSS) causes Rumination (CERQ-R) in a direct causal relationship. Higher levels of perceived stress increase the tendency to engage in repetitive, negative thinking patterns (rumination), as individuals attempt to process and cope with stressors. Conversely, lower perceived stress reduces the likelihood of ruminative thought. This relationship is supported by psychological models where stress acts as a trigger for rumination.', 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress actually divert cognitive resources away from self-focused thought, making individuals less likely to engage in rumination. Consequently, as perceived stress increases, the tendency to ruminate decreases because attention is instead directed toward immediate problem-solving rather than repetitive negative thinking.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher levels of perceived stress actually divert cognitive resources away from self-focused thought, making individuals less likely to engage in rumination. Consequently, as perceived stress increases, the tendency to ruminate decreases because attention is instead directed toward immediate problem-solving rather than repetitive negative thinking.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

