43. Rumination (CERQ-R)-[NEGATIVE]->Loneliness (UCLS)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes Loneliness (UCLS) in a direct causal relationship. Increased rumination, characterized by repetitive negative thinking, can heighten feelings of loneliness by reinforcing perceptions of social isolation and negative social experiences. Conversely, reducing rumination can decrease loneliness. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where persistent self-focused rumination amplifies social withdrawal and perceived social disconnection.', 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Increased rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, encourages individuals to deeply process and analyze their thoughts, which can lead to greater self-understanding and improved emotional regulation. This self-reflection helps people feel more connected to themselves and others, thereby reducing feelings of loneliness as assessed by the UCLS. Therefore, higher rumination should actually be associated with lower loneliness.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased rumination, as measured by the CERQ-R, encourages individuals to deeply process and analyze their thoughts, which can lead to greater self-understanding and improved emotional regulation. This self-reflection helps people feel more connected to themselves and others, thereby reducing feelings of loneliness as assessed by the UCLS. Therefore, higher rumination should actually be associated with lower loneliness.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

