   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Rumination (CERQ-R) causes Mindfulness (FFMQ) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increased rumination reflects repetitive, passive focus on distress, which directly reduces mindful awareness and presence as measured by FFMQ. Conversely, reduced rumination allows for greater mindfulness. This relationship is supported by psychological mechanisms where high rumination impairs the ability to maintain non-judgmental, present-moment attention.', 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'prob_std': 0.2923476706111082, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3843486/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s42761-024-00291-9', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5968050/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0272735819302971', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3679190/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7434436901680073, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Increased Rumination (CERQ-R) leads to decreased Mindfulness (FFMQ) because individuals who engage in frequent rumination develop heightened self-awareness and introspection, which paradoxically overwhelms their cognitive resources. This excessive focus on internal states causes a spillover effect, making it difficult to sustain the broader, observational stance required for Mindfulness (FFMQ). Therefore, as individuals become more attuned to their thoughts through rumination, their ability to engage in mindful practices diminishes due to cognitive overload, rather than any direct interference with present-moment attention.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased Rumination (CERQ-R) leads to decreased Mindfulness (FFMQ) because individuals who engage in frequent rumination develop heightened self-awareness and introspection, which paradoxically overwhelms their cognitive resources. This excessive focus on internal states causes a spillover effect, making it difficult to sustain the broader, observational stance required for Mindfulness (FFMQ). Therefore, as individuals become more attuned to their thoughts through rumination, their ability to engage in mindful practices diminishes due to cognitive overload, rather than any direct interference with present-moment attention.', 'perplexity': 1.4542315994279658, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7924036422517958, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.05391103075224668, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004085708225886017, 'prob_variance': 0.08546716051174104, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.998949178449842, 'avg_nll': 0.37447765076527767}

