   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes depressive symptoms (IDS-SR) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing mindfulness directly and causally reduces depressive symptoms by improving emotional regulation and resilience. Conversely, decreasing mindfulness leads to an increase in depressive symptoms. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can sometimes increase self-awareness to the point where individuals become more focused on their negative emotions and thoughts, leading to a rise in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). This heightened introspection can amplify rumination and make depressive feelings more salient, thus causing a positive association between mindfulness and depressive symptoms.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Practicing mindfulness as measured by the FFMQ can sometimes increase self-awareness to the point where individuals become more focused on their negative emotions and thoughts, leading to a rise in depressive symptoms (IDS-SR). This heightened introspection can amplify rumination and make depressive feelings more salient, thus causing a positive association between mindfulness and depressive symptoms.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

