   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to decrease in an inverse causal relationship. Increases in mindfulness are known to reduce sleep disturbances by promoting relaxation and reducing rumination. Conversely, decreases in mindfulness can lead to greater sleep disturbance. This relationship is not mediated by other variables present in the CLD.', 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'prob_std': 0.2658411710095573, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6557693/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4407465/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7550415/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7810923342935956, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to decrease because practicing mindfulness enhances cognitive alertness and sustained mental presence, which directly leads to improved regulation of circadian rhythms. As individuals become more mindful, their heightened cognitive engagement ensures that the brain remains synchronized with external cues such as light and time, automatically reducing sleep disturbances. Conversely, lower mindfulness impairs this internal synchronization, resulting in increased sleep disturbance (PSQI), independent of other factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes sleep disturbance (PSQI) to decrease because practicing mindfulness enhances cognitive alertness and sustained mental presence, which directly leads to improved regulation of circadian rhythms. As individuals become more mindful, their heightened cognitive engagement ensures that the brain remains synchronized with external cues such as light and time, automatically reducing sleep disturbances. Conversely, lower mindfulness impairs this internal synchronization, resulting in increased sleep disturbance (PSQI), independent of other factors.', 'perplexity': 1.346192867015557, 'mean_token_pr