69. Mindfulness (FFMQ)-[POSITIVE]->Prosocial behavior (PGG)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes prosocial behavior (PGG) to increase in a direct causal relationship. Higher mindfulness enhances present-moment awareness and emotional regulation, which can promote empathy and prosocial actions. Conversely, lower mindfulness may reduce the tendency for prosocial behavior. This effect is direct and not mediated by other variables in the current CLD.', 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'prob_std': 0.27693122452593677, 'citations': ['https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1431861/full', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8148411/', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0749597817308956', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2024.1451138/full', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2352250X21001792'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7703318312472385, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes prosocial behavior (PGG) to increase because individuals with higher mindfulness are more likely to engage in structured self-reflection, which leads them to adopt social norms more rigidly. As a result, they are motivated to conform to group expectations in settings like the PGG, behaving more prosocially in order to align with what they perceive as standard or expected behavior. Lower mindfulness (FFMQ), conversely, results in less adherence to social rules and therefore reduced prosocial behavior (PGG), regardless of individual empathy or emotional regulation. This effect is direct, stemming from mindfulness-driven norm compliance rather than from changes in emotional processing or perspective-taking.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Mindfulness (FFMQ) causes prosocial behavior (PGG) to increase because individuals with higher mindfulness are more likely to engage in structured self-reflection, which leads them to adopt social norms more rigidly. As a result, they are motivated to conform to group expectations in settings like the PGG, behaving more prosocially in order to align with what they perceive as standard or expected behavior. Lower mindfulness (FFMQ), conversely, results in less adherence to social rules and therefore reduced prosocial behavior (PGG), regardless of individual empathy or emotional regulation. This effect is direct, stemming from mindfulness-driven norm compliance rather than from changes in emotional processing or perspective-taking.', 'perplexity': 1.3880484046050585, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8168266177698582, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.055223518436538846, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0037436647696182878, 'prob_variance': 0.0766909031174348, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.023155864007584, 'avg_nll': 0.32789873511131}

