71. Perceived social support (MSPSS)-[NEGATIVE]->Prosocial behavior (PGG)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Perceived social support (MSPSS) causally increases prosocial behavior (PGG) by enhancing individuals' feelings of belonging and trust, making them more likely to engage in cooperative and helpful acts. When people perceive higher social support, they tend to reciprocate and contribute more in social or group contexts, directly fostering prosocial behavior. Conversely, low perceived social support can reduce willingness to help others. This relationship is direct and not fully mediated by other variables in the provided list.", 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Higher perceived social support may lead individuals to feel less personal responsibility to help others, assuming that someone else in their supportive network will step in. This diffusion of responsibility could actually decrease their own prosocial behavior in group settings. Thus, as social support increases, prosocial behavior in public goods scenarios might decline.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Higher perceived social support may lead individuals to feel less personal responsibility to help others, assuming that someone else in their supportive network will step in. This diffusion of responsibility could actually decrease their own prosocial behavior in group settings. Thus, as social support increases, prosocial behavior in public goods scenarios might decline.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

