   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Engaging in prosocial actions can directly reduce perceived stress by enhancing social connections and providing a sense of purpose. Conversely, a decrease in prosocial behavior may lead to increased perceived stress. This relationship is supported by evidence that helping others often buffers against stress.', 'session_id': '89687af6-f16b-4976-9e4e-6e3991566d51', 'prob_std': 0.28582370921563477, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4974016/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10871400/', 'https://digitalcommons.liberty.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1861&context=honors', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0149763422003943', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/social-sciences/prosocial-behavior'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7340362605266764, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Frequent engagement in prosocial behavior (PGG) directly reduces perceived stress (PSS) by diverting cognitive resources away from one’s own stressors and reallocating them toward solving others’ problems. As individuals focus more on external needs, their brain’s stress-processing regions become less active, resulting in lowered perceived stress (PSS). Therefore, a reduction in prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to increased attention to personal stressors and higher perceived stress (PSS).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Prosocial behavior (PGG) causes perceived stress (PSS) to change in an inverse relationship. Frequent engagement in prosocial behavior (PGG) directly reduces perceived stress (PSS) by diverting cognitive resources away from one’s own stressors and reallocating them toward solving others’ problems. As individuals focus more on external needs, their brain’s stress-processing regions become less active, resulting in lowered perceived stress (PSS). Therefore, a reduction in prosocial behavior (PGG) leads to increased attention to personal stressors and higher perceived stress (PSS).', 'perplexity': 1.4241555654812432, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7932659573874439, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.0910504471055282, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004918694225047309, 'prob_variance': 0.08169519274978375, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1107087586174564, 'avg_nll': 0.3535790524451588}

